      "id": "GENUS_ARTAMUS",
      "name": "Artamus",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "The woodswallows — a genus of highly social, aerial insectivores with a superficial resemblance to swallows, for which they are named. They have broad, triangular wings, short tails, and stout, finch-like bills adapted for catching insects on the wing. They are among the most gregarious of all Australasian passerines, often packing tightly together on exposed branches in large, noisy flocks.",
      "lineage": "Woodswallows & Butcherbirds",
